WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.836
Hello?
What's your favorite
scary movie?

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.504
Who is this?

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:07.382
[deep voice]
What's your favorite
scary movie?

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:09.175
Oh, Dr. Z,
is this you?

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.010
You really have to stop
with these prank calls.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.680
But, serious, what's
your favorite scary movie?

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.181
Okay, we can talk
about that later,

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.766
but I'm filming
an episode right now.

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:18.268
Okay, sorry,
good luck.

00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:20.311
Okay, bye!
Bye!

00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:22.272
If you've attended
a sleepover party

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:24.190
anytime in the last
50 years,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.942
chances are high
that you've encountered

00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:27.235
a prank call like this.

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.154
Maybe you've even
made one yourself.

00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:30.488
But for thousands of years

00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:32.198
before the invention
of the telephone,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.992
pranks fell under the purview
of a force

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:36.369
even more powerful
than rowdy teens.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.830
It was left
to the Trickster Gods.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.542
[intro music]

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.544
From Maui
in the Southern Hemisphere

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:46.087
to Amaguq in the North,

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:48.339
Trickster Gods and spirits
can be found in mythologies

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:49.883
across the globe,

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.385
and their stories are some
of the most fascinating ones,

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:55.096
like the Chinese tale
of Sun Wukong.

00:00:55.096 --> 00:00:57.015
Also known as the Monkey King,

00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:59.142
Sun Wukong used
his super-human strength

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:01.102
and impressive
shape-shifting abilities

00:01:01.102 --> 00:01:02.979
to pester the other gods.

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:04.981
Once, he crashed
a heavenly banquet

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:07.650
that he was rudely
not invited to,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.320
drank all the wine,
and then drunkenly stole

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.113
a bunch of immortality pills.

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:13.698
For his misbehavior,

00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:15.909
the Monkey King was
imprisoned under a mountain

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.535
for 500 years,

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:21.080
and his escape provides fodder
for countless modern stories.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.707
On the other side
of the world,

00:01:22.707 --> 00:01:25.210
the Hopi people
of the American Southwest

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.753
told tales of Kokopelli.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:28.379
According to one source,

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:31.090
Kokopelli is described
as a lascivious troublemaker

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.259
whose obscene behavior
provides an outlet

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.304
for people's less socially
acceptable desires

00:01:36.304 --> 00:01:38.932
and reminds them
how they should behave.

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:40.433
This flute-playing
Trickster God

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.143
is also a fertility deity

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:44.771
who makes his own fun
as he travels the region

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:47.774
to spread corn seed
and encourage pregnancy,

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:51.236
sometimes through wild parties
and erotic dances.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.780
This naughty figure
is so popular in the Southwest

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.365
that his image can be found

00:01:55.365 --> 00:01:58.076
adorning countless
pieces of art and souvenirs.

00:01:58.076 --> 00:02:00.119
And of course,
the Norse people gave us

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:02.831
what might be the most
widely known Trickster of all--

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:05.208
Loki, the god of mischief.

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:08.169
Much of what Marvel reveals
about Loki onscreen

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.004
is faithful to the old myths,

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:13.716
like his chaotic nature and
his long-held beef with Thor.

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:15.885
Even alligator Loki
has some foundation

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:17.387
in the old Eddas,

00:02:17.387 --> 00:02:19.472
which mention Loki's
shape-shifting powers.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.851
But the MCU's interpretation
of Loki is just that--

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:24.394
their interpretation.

00:02:24.394 --> 00:02:25.770
In the original myths,

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.522
Thor and Loki
aren't even brothers,

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:30.400
so don't expect to be
an expert in Norse mythology

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.777
after watching Tom Hiddleston
charm his way

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:34.863
to the Avengers'
bad sides.

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:36.155
By the end of this video,

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.074
I'll tell you about
another Marvel superhero

00:02:38.074 --> 00:02:40.994
inspired by a Trickster God,
but first, let's analyze

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.872
why the Trickster is
so common around the world.

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:47.292
It has to do with why myths
were told in the first place.

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:49.711
Before the Internet
and the scientific method

00:02:49.711 --> 00:02:51.963
and even the concept
of writing things down,

00:02:51.963 --> 00:02:55.008
humans told stories
for a few different purposes--

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.801
to explain the natural world,

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:59.012
to educate, and to entertain.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.680
Myths, like monsters,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.849
were created
to explain natural phenomena

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.643
and reflect human understanding
of the world around them,

00:03:05.643 --> 00:03:07.228
and throughout time immemorial,

00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:10.440
one thing that humans have
definitely known about nature

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:11.858
is that it's tricky.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.985
Storms come
when you least expect them,

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:17.196
animals steal food
and other valuable resources,

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.574
and don't even get me started
on fire--

00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:22.535
a bright light that provides
warmth and prepares your food

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:23.912
but can also burn you

00:03:23.912 --> 00:03:26.372
and magically
turns wood into ash?

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.875
Sounds like
a very elaborate prank to me.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.044
A Trickster Deity
is a convenient way

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:32.754
to explain these events.

00:03:32.754 --> 00:03:34.714
Indeed, in many mythologies,

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:38.259
it's a Trickster who teaches
humanity how to harness fire.

00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:40.720
Myths were also used
to codify social norms

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.680
for children and adults alike.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.808
In mythology, the Trickster
often faces consequences

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:47.727
for their nefarious hijinks,

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.729
like when Sun Wukong
was imprisoned

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.273
for eating the very
peaches of immortality

00:03:52.273 --> 00:03:54.108
that he was assigned
to protect.

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:57.487
These peaches, in addition
to the pills he stole before,

00:03:57.487 --> 00:04:00.823
made Sun Wukong
extra invincible.

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:03.076
This lets everyone
who hears the story

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:05.745
know that an immortal god
could not run their punishment,

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.413
and neither can you.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.583
And finally, Trickster stories
are fun and funny,

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.086
just like an entertaining myth
is supposed to be.

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:16.798
The chaos and randomness
inherent to a Trickster persona

00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:18.508
often make us laugh.

00:04:18.508 --> 00:04:21.344
Psychologists who study humor
have found a few theories

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:23.054
about what
makes something funny,

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:25.848
and incongruity
is one such theory.

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:28.226
We find something funny
when there's a mismatch

00:04:28.226 --> 00:04:30.687
between our expectations
and reality,

00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:32.647
and randomness creates
the opportunity

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:35.358
for that
hilarious juxtaposition.

00:04:35.358 --> 00:04:36.943
In one Norse myth,

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.445
the giantess Skadi
demands to be compensated

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.614
for her father's murder
and insists

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.575
that one of the gods
needs to make her laugh.

00:04:44.575 --> 00:04:46.911
Loki is apparently
the only Norse god

00:04:46.911 --> 00:04:48.997
with a sense of humor,
so he tied one end

00:04:48.997 --> 00:04:52.125
of a rope to a goat
and the other to a very...

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.460
sensitive part of his body

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.672
and then squealed with pain
as the goat ran away.

00:04:57.672 --> 00:05:00.675
It must have been a
you-had-to-be-there kind of joke

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:02.969
because Skadi
was appropriately amused

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.013
and agreed
not to kill the gods.

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:06.514
So Tricksters can be found

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:08.474
in so many
different mythologies

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.810
because nature
is violent everywhere,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.979
and the crafty qualities
of the Trickster

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:15.481
make this particular archetype
the perfect vehicle

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:18.359
for delivering moral
or practical lessons

00:05:18.359 --> 00:05:20.236
in a fun, memorable package.

00:05:20.236 --> 00:05:23.281
Across cultures, Tricksters
share many characteristics.

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:26.409
They're unpredictable,
are often able to shapeshift,

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:28.077
and they rely
on their quick wit

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:29.537
and penchant for mischief

00:05:29.537 --> 00:05:31.789
to get them
out of hairy situations.

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:34.625
One Trickster that embodies
all of these qualities

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:38.379
is Anansi, the Trickster god
of knowledge and stories

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:40.214
who originated with
the Akan people

00:05:40.214 --> 00:05:42.008
of Ghana in West Africa.

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:44.594
Christianity has been
the dominant religion in Ghana

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:46.054
for nearly a century,

00:05:46.054 --> 00:05:48.848
but traditional Akan religion
is polytheistic.

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:51.309
Their pantheon is ruled
by a supreme Creator God

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:54.437
who goes by many names,
sometimes Nyame.

00:05:54.437 --> 00:05:56.981
Second to Nyame
is Asase Yaa,

00:05:56.981 --> 00:05:58.858
a goddess of the earth,
and together,

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.861
they have many children and
messengers of lesser power

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.362
known as Abosom.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.907
Amongst some,
Anansi is the most important

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:07.408
of these children.

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:10.244
Often depicted as a spider
with a man's head,

00:06:10.244 --> 00:06:11.996
or a man with eight legs,

00:06:11.996 --> 00:06:16.375
Anansi plays a key role in
some fundamental Akan stories.

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:17.877
According to one,

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.797
the human world was
originally bereft of stories

00:06:20.797 --> 00:06:22.757
until Anansi got bored one day

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:25.551
and decided to buy them
from his dad, Nyame.

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:28.262
The Sky God said that Anansi
could have his stories

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:30.014
for a steep price.

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:31.599
Anansi had to bring him four

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:33.768
of the hardest creatures
to catch--

00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:35.061
Onini the python,

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:36.896
the hive of Mmoboro hornets,

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.065
Osebo the leopard,
and a fairy.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:42.860
But Anansi uses his cunning
and his targets' weaknesses

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.737
to capture each one.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:48.407
Impressed, Nyame granted Anansi
ownership of his stories,

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:51.661
and Anansi shared them
with humanity.

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:53.704
But lest you think
that makes him a good guy,

00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:56.165
there's another story
where Anansi tries to horde

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:59.252
all the wisdom in the world
by trapping it in a pot

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.462
and sticking it
at the top of a tree.

00:07:01.462 --> 00:07:04.423
Except the pot
was much bigger than Anansi,

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:06.759
and he had trouble
carrying it to the top

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:08.344
until his son, Ntikuma,

00:07:08.344 --> 00:07:10.680
suggested he place it
on his back.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.181
Anansi was so shocked

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:14.308
that he hadn't thought
of the answer himself

00:07:14.308 --> 00:07:16.435
that the pot slipped
from his grip.

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.104
The pot shattered
on the ground,

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:20.815
and a storm carried away
the wisdom inside

00:07:20.815 --> 00:07:22.608
to the rest of the world.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.193
In yet another story,

00:07:24.193 --> 00:07:25.987
Anansi brings disease
to the world

00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:27.947
in an angry act of revenge

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:31.117
after his father took
all of his wives from him.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:32.577
In Nyame's defense,

00:07:32.577 --> 00:07:35.621
Anansi did promise to bring him
a beautiful wife

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.874
in exchange for one
of his delicious sheep,

00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:41.294
and then used that sheep
to woo several new wives

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:43.087
who he kept for himself.

00:07:43.087 --> 00:07:44.547
When the Atlantic slave trade

00:07:44.547 --> 00:07:46.549
forced West Africans
from their home

00:07:46.549 --> 00:07:48.384
and enslaved them
across an ocean,

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:50.636
they took stories of Anansi
with them.

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:53.556
They held onto him
as a symbol of resistance,

00:07:53.556 --> 00:07:55.057
as a reminder that,

00:07:55.057 --> 00:07:56.976
with a little bit
of cunning and resilience,

00:07:56.976 --> 00:07:59.187
even something as small
as a spider

00:07:59.187 --> 00:08:02.106
can escape
most tough situations.

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:05.026
Now stories of Anansi
have spread and morphed

00:08:05.026 --> 00:08:07.320
across the Caribbean
and the Americas,

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.072
making their way into plenty
of popular media,

00:08:10.072 --> 00:08:13.534
like Neil Gaiman's Mr. Nancy
from "American Gods,"

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:15.161
or Spider-Man.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:18.664
That's right--it's Marvel canon
that in an alternate universe,

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:20.666
Earth 7082,
to be precise,

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:24.003
the very first Spider-Man
was Kwaku Anansi.

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:26.088
Whether it's displayed
on the silver screen,

00:08:26.088 --> 00:08:27.757
told around a campfire,

00:08:27.757 --> 00:08:29.508
or painted on the wall
of a cave,

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:32.303
Trickster stories remind us
to roll with the punches

00:08:32.303 --> 00:08:33.888
that life throws at us

00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:36.724
and to face obstacles
with cunning instead of force.

00:08:36.724 --> 00:08:39.560
But Tricksters'
most important characteristic

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.354
is their unpredictability.

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:44.357
You never know if a Trickster
in a story will be the hero

00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:46.525
or the villain
or merely the fool

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:48.110
providing comic relief.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.738
Their flexibility is
what makes them so effective

00:08:50.738 --> 00:08:51.989
and appealing.

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:53.658
And if you start
looking for them,

00:08:53.658 --> 00:08:55.076
you'll see
Tricksters everywhere,

00:08:55.076 --> 00:08:56.869
from Prometheus
tricking the gods

00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:58.204
into giving us fire,

00:08:58.204 --> 00:08:59.997
to the Roadrunner
"beep beeping" his way

00:08:59.997 --> 00:09:02.875
through a world where the laws
of physics don't mean a thing.

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:05.378
Comment below with
your favorite Trickster figure,

00:09:05.378 --> 00:09:07.672
and remember:
prank responsibly.

00:09:07.672 --> 00:09:10.716
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

